### What is the difference between get() and join()?

Both methods block the calling thread until the CompletableFuture completes and return the result. The difference is how they handle exceptions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class GetVsJoinDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<String> cf = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> "SETTLED:TRD-001");

        // get(): checked exceptions, needs try-catch
        try {
            String result = cf.get();
            System.out.println("get: " + result);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        } catch (ExecutionException e) {
            System.out.println("Cause: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
        }

        // join(): unchecked, no try-catch needed
        String result = cf.join();
        System.out.println("join: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
get: SETTLED:TRD-001
join: SETTLED:TRD-001
```

### Why is join() better in streams and lambdas?

Lambda expressions cannot throw checked exceptions. Using get() inside a stream's map() causes a compile error. join() works cleanly because it throws only unchecked exceptions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.List;

public class JoinInStreamDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = List.of(
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "SETTLED:TRD-001"),
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "SETTLED:TRD-002"),
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "SETTLED:TRD-003")
        );

        // With join(): clean!
        List<String> results = futures.stream()
            .map(CompletableFuture::join) // no checked exception
            .toList();

        results.forEach(System.out::println);

        // With get(): COMPILE ERROR in lambda!
        // futures.stream().map(f -> f.get()).toList(); // won't compile
    }
}
```

```text
SETTLED:TRD-001
SETTLED:TRD-002
SETTLED:TRD-003
```

### How does the timeout variant work?

get() has a timeout variant. join() does not, but you can combine orTimeout() with join() in Java 9+.

```java
// get() with timeout
try {
    String result = cf.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
} catch (TimeoutException e) {
    System.out.println("Timed out");
} catch (ExecutionException | InterruptedException e) {
    // handle
}

// join() with timeout workaround (Java 9+)
String result = cf.orTimeout(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS).join();
// If CF does not complete in 5s: CompletionException(TimeoutException)
```

### How do get() and join() compare?

| Feature | get() | join() |
|---|---|---|
| Exception type | Checked (ExecutionException, InterruptedException) | Unchecked (CompletionException) |
| Try-catch required | Yes | No |
| Timeout variant | get(timeout, unit) | None (use orTimeout().join()) |
| In streams/lambdas | Does not compile | Works cleanly |
| Exception wrapper | ExecutionException | CompletionException |

### How do you handle exceptions from join()?

```java
try {
    String result = cf.join();
} catch (CompletionException e) {
    Throwable cause = e.getCause(); // original exception
    if (cause instanceof TimeoutException) {
        System.out.println("Timed out");
    } else {
        System.out.println("Failed: " + cause.getMessage());
    }
}
```

### When should you use get() vs join()?

- **join()**: in stream pipelines, lambda chains, and anywhere checked exceptions are inconvenient. Default choice.
- **get(timeout, unit)**: for individual critical calls where you need a timeout deadline.
- **get()**: when you specifically want checked exception handling in a try-catch block.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: using get() in a stream
futures.stream().map(f -> f.get()).toList(); // COMPILE ERROR

// CORRECT: use join()
futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList();

// WRONG: join() with no timeout on external call
cf.join(); // blocks forever if external service hangs

// CORRECT: add timeout
cf.orTimeout(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS).join();
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does join() restore the interrupt flag? No, join() does not throw InterruptedException.
- Can you use join() instead of get() everywhere? Yes, except when you need the timeout overload of get().
- What is the cause of CompletionException? It wraps the original exception thrown inside the CompletableFuture pipeline.
